2. Frame your action as a safety control

“When an AE Title doesn’t match the approved configuration, the connection is held. That’s a safeguard to prevent unvalidated or misconfigured systems from sending images into PACS, which could lead to incorrect routing or data integrity issues.”

3. Be ready to explain your workflow simply

“My workflow is based on the approved Modality Interface request. I validate incoming connections against that configuration. If everything matches, traffic is allowed. If it doesn’t match, it’s placed on hold until the configuration can be verified.”

4. Be clear about your dependency (this protects you)

“I rely on the Modality Interface request and coordination from BIO-MED and the department to know what configuration to expect. That’s what I use to validate and enable routing. If what’s observed doesn’t match what was submitted, I can’t safely allow it through.”

5. If they bring up patient impact

“I understand there was a delay, and that’s not ideal. At the same time, allowing an unrecognized or unvalidated AE Title into PACS could result in misrouted or incorrect studies, which also presents a patient safety and data integrity risk. The hold is intended to prevent that.”

6. If they ask about manual intervention

“Once the situation was understood and there was awareness of clinical impact, I facilitated delivery of the affected studies to ensure they were available for interpretation, while the configuration discrepancy remained under review. I do not bypass validation for unknown configurations, but I can support delivery once the context is confirmed.”
